# Generated by Cargo
# will have compiled files and executables
/target
**/target
.DS_Store

# These are backup files generated by rustfmt
**/*.rs.bk

# IDE
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo

# OS
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log

# Build artifacts
dist/
build/

# Node modules (if using any JS tools)
node_modules/

# Environment variables
.env
.env.local

# Temporary files
*.tmp
*.bak

# Dioxus specific
.dioxus/

# Windows build directory (created by our script)
../scrabble-windows-build/

# Claude Code local settings/session state
# `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/`: git cannot re-include anything inside an
# excluded directory, so excluding the contents is what lets skills back in.
.claude/*
# Skills encode this project's process rather than one machine's settings, so
# they are versioned — docs/3.6's test is that what survives a rebuild
# elsewhere is what has been delivered.
!.claude/skills/
# The two hooks are tooling by the same test, and tooling is an artefact
# whether it runs in production or not (owner, 2026-09-03). They were invisible
# to the repo until then, which is how turn-check.sh came to be filtering on a
# convention that nothing had written for weeks. settings.json stays ignored:
# it is one machine's permissions and absolute paths, not process.
!.claude/turn-check.sh
!.claude/inbox-hook.sh

# PID files written by scripts/services.sh
.pid.server
.pid.web

# Local dev SQLite database — recreated fresh by persistence::migrate()
# on server startup (create_if_missing). Tracking this in git meant a
# `git checkout`/`git reset` on it silently reverted both data *and*
# schema together (no real migration system — see docs/operations.md),
# which twice caused "no such column" errors after an innocuous-looking
# checkout. Local dev data is disposable; there's nothing here worth
# committing.
data/*.sqlite3
data/*.sqlite3-wal
data/*.sqlite3-shm

# Playwright E2E (Node lives only under e2e/)
e2e/test-results/
e2e/playwright-report/
e2e/.last-run.json

# Local dev DB backups (see scripts / 3.4 Production Environment)
data/backups/


# Build artefacts: one tar.gz per commit, shared between environments so
# rehearsal and production load the same bytes (#214). A cache — losing one
# costs a rebuild of a known commit.
artifacts/

# Python bytecode from importing scripts/actions.py as a module
__pycache__/

# Per-move benchmark detail, one file per run, named <epoch>-<host>.csv by
# engine_timing_bench.rs. They are run artefacts: the summary row in
# engine_timing_results.csv is the record that is kept, and these exist to be
# read once while judging it. Left untracked and unignored they made the tree
# dirty after every rehearsal benchmark, which is a release step, so
# verify.sh's clean-tree check failed for a reason nobody had to act on.
#
# The digit prefix is what distinguishes them: moves/wide-5x5-c2281dc.csv is
# tracked deliberately, for the analysis in docs/2.3.
crates/server-game/examples/moves/[0-9]*.csv
